#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/id #uuid "5da3337e-31d2-5f2d-b236-60088b4bf64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3524" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} u &= 1 - \\sin^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (-\\frac{4}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{4^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{16}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u + v) &= \\cos u\\cos v - \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17}) - (-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{45}{85} + \\frac{32}{85} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{13}{85}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "8877f6b8-8914-5022-9ec6-d4e8e0d3d159" :question/math-academy-id "e-9992" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} v &= 1 - \\sin^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{9}{15})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{9^{2}}{15^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{225}{225} - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{144}{225}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{12}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u - v) &= \\cos u\\cos v + \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15}) + (\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{75} + \\frac{36}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{72}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b73259-15cc-551b-bb72-39d334dc51a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183582} {:answer/id #uuid "c762bf12-b1e6-5249-9aac-e301c9001f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{74}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183583} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3191d0-ff2b-5919-b490-9a88c6f906dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183584} {:answer/id #uuid "d3da2a44-a5fb-5096-84d9-892bdc2b8028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183585} {:answer/id #uuid "01304835-f6c5-5d9a-ae80-b8f633aa7c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183586}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dc9f13b7-9826-57a7-80fe-f4b56f9fa1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183581}] :question/id #uuid "9f39d2c0-c3fb-5d82-bb15-c8596eec6f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-74457" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos u = \\frac{4}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{15}{17}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos (u - v)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7b49492-c8b0-59f3-a071-2d075e233908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$13$\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"computing-the-cosine-of-a-composite-angle-using-angles-in-the-second-third-and-fourth-quadrants\"></a>" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183588} {:answer/id #uuid "be2d14cb-2c12-5b67-bada-e1d4f1f7233a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183589} {:answer/id #uuid "3bccb94b-7af2-546b-b781-c914509ae500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183590} {:answer/id #uuid "418b04df-0fb2-5061-b6d8-fc3a5f74a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183591} {:answer/id #uuid "62d7d50f-de5f-5a67-8714-8f51c777f5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183592}] :answer-field/id #uuid "14588477-f694-5abf-9bf2-666911921ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183587}] :question/id #uuid "c420f71d-937d-5553-a82e-dad726247670" :question/math-academy-id "q-74459" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}, \\sin v = \\frac{3}{5}, \\cos v = \\frac{4}{5}$.\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\cos (u + v) = \\frac{a\\sqrt{6} - b}{35}$,\ncalculate the value of $a + b$."}]}
